// Sonic's insta-shield — the ability hook AND the flash it draws.
//
// ONE MODULE for both halves, deliberately. They are one feature sharing one
// three-value state machine: the hook opens the attack window and spawns the flash,
// the flash closes the window on its last animation frame, and neither is meaningful
// without the other. Splitting them would put a two-line `use` edge between twelve
// instructions and their visual — and the tree's own precedent is the ring sparkle,
// whose header records that "the self-contained file is the honest unit".
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ABILITY (S3K `Sonic_ShieldMoves`, sonic3k.asm:23401-23486, from the press
// test down). The seam in player_air.emp already owns WHEN an ability fires (a
// FRESH jump press while already airborne, with the jump buffer unspent so the
// launch press cannot trigger it); CharDef_Sonic.cd_ability owns WHAT fires.
//
// AIRBORNE IS NOT ENOUGH — THE PRESS MUST HAVE COME FROM A JUMP, and that gate is
// this proc's own step (0). It is not optional garnish: `Sonic_ShieldMoves` is
// reachable in S3K from exactly ONE place, `Sonic_JumpHeight`, whose first two
// instructions are
//
//      Sonic_JumpHeight:
//              tst.b   jumping(a0)     ; is Sonic jumping?
//              beq.s   Sonic_UpVelCap  ; if not, branch      (sonic3k.asm:23369-23370)
//
// and `Sonic_UpVelCap` rts's without ever reaching the shield moves. So in the
// reference, `jumping != 0` is a HARD PRECONDITION of the insta-shield, and the rule
// it produces is player-visible: launched by a spring, no insta-shield (the spring
// does `clr.b jumping(a1)`, :47728 / :48144 / :48218 / :48307); knocked back by a hit,
// no insta-shield (`HurtCharacter` calls `Player_TouchFloor`, which clears it,
// :21093 -> :24373); walked off a ledge, no insta-shield (nothing ever set it);
// roll-jumped, YES (`Sonic_Jump` sets `jumping` at :23335 BEFORE the roll branch);
// broke a monitor mid-jump, YES (`Touch_Monitor` reverses y_vel and never touches
// `jumping`). The owner's report — "sonic can still insta shield when not in ball
// form" — is the ledge case, and it is what this gate closes.
//
// THIS ENGINE HAS NO `jumping` BYTE AND NEEDS NONE. `PlayerV.player_state` already
// discriminates: PSTATE_JUMP is "airborne curled FROM A JUMP" and PSTATE_ROLLJUMP is
// the same with the air-control lockout, while the two air states that are NOT from a
// jump carry their own values — PSTATE_AIR (uncurled: ledge walk-off, debug-fly exit,
// Player_Init) and PSTATE_AIRBALL (curled but rolled off a ledge, or the spindash's
// floor-vanish). The pair {PSTATE_JUMP, PSTATE_ROLLJUMP} is therefore S3K's `jumping`
// exactly, and the equivalence was established by enumerating every WRITER of
// player_state rather than every reader: `Player_SetState` (player_common.emp:1247)
// is the sole transition writer — its own header says so — plus `Player_Init`'s boot
// `clr.b` (:516, which writes PSTATE_GROUND). The complete set of sites that install
// one of the two is Player_Jump (player_ground.emp:1047/1050, the real jump) and
// Climb_JumpOff (player_climb.emp:380, Knuckles-only, and S3K sets `jumping` there
// too, :31428). Nothing else installs them, so no non-jump launcher can smuggle a
// player into the allowed set. That is a fact about TODAY's tree and it is cheap to
// keep true: springs, badnik bounces and knockback are all `rts` stubs in
// engine/objects/collision.emp — when they are built they must install PSTATE_AIR /
// PSTATE_AIRBALL (S3K's launchers clear `jumping`; only BubbleShield_Bounce, :24421,
// deliberately sets it), and a launcher that reaches for PSTATE_JUMP is choosing to
// grant the insta-shield.
//
// ONE S3K GATE IS STILL MISSING, and it is stated rather than hidden: the same
// `Sonic_JumpHeight` reaches `Sonic_ShieldMoves` only past `cmp.w y_vel(a0),d1 /
// ble.w`, with d1 = -$400 (-$200 underwater) — i.e. the insta-shield is unavailable
// while still rising faster than the release cap, roughly the first 12 frames of a
// jump at our PHYS_JUMP_FORCE/gravity. BOTH SIBLING ABILITIES ALREADY HAVE IT
// (Ability_TailsFlight and Ability_KnuxGlide open with `move.w y_vel(a0),d1 / cmp.w
// PBLK_RELEASE_CAP(a4),d1 / blt`, citing Knux_JumpHeight :32520-32522), so this is an
// omission specific to the insta-shield rather than an engine-wide simplification —
// and a4 is already an AbilityHook input, so it costs ~10 bytes. Not built here
// because it is a FEEL change nobody reported, and one that would move the owner's
// recorded replay net (ojz_fixture's airborne press sits ~11 ticks after its jump,
// right on the window's edge) — and re-stamping that net is his call, not a parcel's.
// Booked in docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md with the arithmetic.
//
// WHAT THE REFERENCE DOES THAT IS NOT HERE, and why: S3K reaches `Sonic_InstaShield`
// only after a branch tree — Super/Hyper transform, hyper dash, and the
// fire/lightning/bubble double jumps — each of which takes the press instead and sets
// `double_jump_flag = 1` itself. None of that exists in this engine (no shields, no
// Super), so that whole tree collapses to ONE thing: the suppression predicate below,
// which asks "does the player carry a barrier?" and today always answers no. When
// shields land they become the branches; the gate does not move.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE FLASH (S3K `Obj_InstaShield`, sonic3k.asm:34570-34630). There it is a
// PERSISTENT object living in a dedicated Shield slot, parked on an invisible idle
// animation and told to play its attack by a write to `(Shield+anim)`. This engine
// has no reserved shield slot and no idle-forever objects, so the flash is spawned
// per use out of the Effect pool and retires itself through `AF_DELETE` — the
// dust/sparkle lifetime. Everything else about it is S3K's: it inherits the player's
// position and facing every frame, streams its own DPLC into its own VRAM window, and
// ends the attack window by writing the player's flag at its last script entry.
//
// A FOLLOWER, NOT A FIRE-AND-FORGET DROP. Sonic moves up to ~16 px per frame and the
// flash is drawn centred on him for 6 visible frames, so a position written once at
// spawn (the ring sparkle's shape) would visibly trail him. It re-reads the player,
// exactly as the charge dust does.
//
// The player pointer lives in the flash's OWN sst_custom overlay rather than in
// `parent_ptr`, following dust_spindash.emp for its three reasons: a non-zero
// parent_ptr makes Draw_Sprite dereference the parent every frame to test
// RF_MULTISPRITE (children.emp:630-635); parent_ptr carries a documented invariant
// that its writer also links the object into the parent's sibling chain, and the
// Tails appendage uses `sibling_ptr != 0` as its sole presence test — a second child
// kind on a player slot would make a live flash look like a live pair of tails; and
// holding it here needs no Player_1 label, so it is 2P-ready.
//
// The state machine's three values, its landing reset and the attacking predicate are
// documented at INSTASHIELD_READY in games/sonic4/config/constants.emp.
// Design note: docs/superpowers/notes/2026-08-26-instashield-design.md.
module games.sonic4.player_instashield in player_instashield

use engine.objects.sst.{Sst}
use engine.objects.objdef.{vram_art, vram_bytes}
use engine.objects.dplc.{dplc_peak_tiles, dplc_peak_entries, DPLC_ENTRY_RESERVE,
                         DPLC_ADDRESSABLE_TILES}
use engine.constants.{RF_PRIORITY_SHIFT, TILE_SIZE, AF_DELETE, AF_SET_FIELD,
                      ST_XFLIP, DMA_DEFERRABLE_SLOTS}
use games.sonic4.player_common.{PlayerV}
use games.sonic4.sound_ids.{SFXID_INSTASHIELD}
use games.sonic4.constants.{PSTATE_JUMP, PSTATE_ROLLJUMP,
                            VRAM_INSTA_SHIELD, VRAM_TEST_SONIC,
                            EFFECT_INSTA_SHIELD_BAND,
                            INSTASHIELD_ATTACKING, INSTASHIELD_SPENT,
                            INSTASHIELD_SUPPRESS_MASK}

// -----------------------------------------------
// INSTASHIELD_ABILITY_WH — the ABILITY-STATE COLLISION BOX this ability needs: NONE.
//
// Read by CharDef_Sonic (games/sonic4/player/sonic.emp), the way CharDef_Knuckles
// reads KNUX_ABILITY_RADIUS: the record's box is a fact about the ability, so it is
// named where the ability is, not left as a bare 0 in a table.
//
// And it is a RULING, not an absence. cd_ability_wh feeds set_ability_size, which
// writes size_wh_off() = Sst.width_pixels/height_pixels — and player_sensors halves
// those into the TERRAIN sensor radii (:343-346, :547). Knuckles' 10x10 is a physics
// box. S3K's insta-shield expansion is a 48x48 box that exists ONLY inside
// TouchResponse and never touches y_radius/x_radius, so routing it through here would
// give Sonic a 48x48 terrain footprint for 14 frames of every insta-shield — he would
// stand 24 px above the floor and jam in every corridor. The expansion is booked
// against the damage system with its arithmetic (docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md; design note
// §1.5/§2.4), not built here and NOT smuggled through this field.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub const INSTASHIELD_ABILITY_WH = 0

// ===========================================================================
// THE ABILITY
// ===========================================================================

// -----------------------------------------------
// instashield_suppressed — THE BARRIER PREDICATE, and the one seam the shield
// work has to fill in.
//
// Leaves the Z flag SET when the player carries nothing that would take the press
// instead (i.e. the insta-shield is allowed), CLEAR when a barrier or invincibility
// suppresses it. `dest` is scratch.
//
// It cannot fire today and that is stated rather than hidden: nothing writes
// PlayerV.status_secondary — Player_Init clears it and it has no other writer
// tree-wide — so the mask always reads 0. The instruction is here anyway because it
// is the reference's own gate and because "where does the shield check go?" should
// have exactly one answer with a name on it. A shield system adds WRITERS to
// status_secondary and changes nothing here.
//
// MODULE-PRIVATE ON PURPOSE (not `pub`): a comptime fn's free names resolve at the
// CALL SITE (docs/EMP_PITFALLS.md §2), and both `PlayerV` and
// INSTASHIELD_SUPPRESS_MASK are `use`-imported here, so they would NOT travel to a
// caller in another module — silently, with no diagnostic. One caller, one module.
comptime fn instashield_suppressed(sst: Reg, dest: Reg) -> Code {
        return asm {
                move.b  PlayerV.status_secondary({sst}), {dest}
                andi.b  #INSTASHIELD_SUPPRESS_MASK, {dest}
        }
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Ability_InstaShield — CharDef_Sonic.cd_ability.
//
// S3K's order is preserved exactly — the `jumping` precondition ahead of everything,
// then the one-shot, then the roll-jump cancel, then the suppression test — including
// the fact that the cancel happens BEFORE the suppression test and so still happens
// when the insta-shield itself is suppressed, and AFTER the one-shot and so does NOT
// happen on a second press in the same airborne stretch (S3K's `tst.b
// double_jump_flag / bne` sits ahead of its `bclr`, sonic3k.asm:23402/23408).
//
// THE ROLL-JUMP CANCEL IS A STATE CHANGE, NOT A BIT CLEAR. S3K writes
// `bclr #Status_RollJump,status(a0)`; this engine has no such bit — the air-control
// lockout is AIRF_INPUT_LOCK, which PState_RollJump's preamble puts in d6
// (player_air.emp:70-71, 92), i.e. it is a property of the STATE. PSTATE_ROLLJUMP
// and PSTATE_JUMP are identical in every other respect (same AIRF_RELEASE_CAP, same
// PHook_AirBallEnter), so the transition is precisely "lift the lock".
//
// It is GATED on actually being in ROLLJUMP, which S3K does not need to be: a
// `bclr` on a clear bit is a no-op, but an unconditional Player_SetState(PSTATE_JUMP)
// would run PHook_AirBallEnter and CURL an uncurled faller. Same effect, different
// precondition, because the mechanisms differ.
//
// ONE FRAME OF DEVIATION, STATED. S3K's bclr takes effect the same frame, because
// Sonic_ChgJumpDir re-tests Status_RollJump after Sonic_JumpHeight returns. Here the
// current frame's lockout is already latched in the CALLER's d6, and the AbilityHook
// contract (clobbers d0-d2/a1-a2) forbids touching it — d6 must survive this call.
// So air control returns on the NEXT frame. Widening the hook contract to let an
// ability write d6 would be a seam change under the replay gate; booked, not built.
//
// In:  a0 = player SST, a4 = the slot's player block (unused here, preserved)
// Out: none
// Clobbers: d0-d2, a1-a2 — the AbilityHook contract verbatim (Player_SetState's
//           d1-d2/a1-a2, plus d0 which carries the target state into it; d1 carries
//           the player_state byte from step (0) to step (2) and is then reused by the
//           suppression predicate).
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc Ability_InstaShield (a0: *Sst) clobbers(d0-d2/a1-a2) preserves(a0) {
        // (0) S3K's `tst.b jumping(a0) / beq` — the airborne frame must have come
        // from a JUMP, not from a ledge walk-off, a launcher or a knockback. See the
        // module header for the derivation and for why {JUMP, ROLLJUMP} IS `jumping`.
        //
        // FIRST, ahead of the one-shot, because that is where the reference puts it:
        // `Sonic_JumpHeight` refuses before `Sonic_ShieldMoves` is even entered, so
        // the `jumping` test outranks `double_jump_flag`, the press test and the
        // roll-jump cancel alike. Refusing here leaves PlayerV.instashield untouched,
        // which is what keeps the one-shot's re-arm set complete: a refused press
        // writes nothing, so nothing can be left stuck for the landing to clear.
        //
        // Two explicit compares rather than an unsigned range test on
        // PSTATE_JUMP..PSTATE_ROLLJUMP: the range form would silently start admitting
        // a third state the day somebody renumbers, and this pair is 2 bytes dearer.
        // d1 holds the state byte across to step (2), which used to re-read it.
        move.b  PlayerV.player_state(a0), d1
        cmpi.b  #PSTATE_JUMP, d1
        beq     .from_jump
        cmpi.b  #PSTATE_ROLLJUMP, d1
        bne     .done                           // AIR / AIRBALL / anything else: the
                                                // press did not come out of a jump
    .from_jump:

        // (1) THE ONE-SHOT. Any non-READY value refuses: ATTACKING means a flash is
        // already running, SPENT means this airborne stretch already had its one.
        // Only a landing re-arms it (PHook_GroundEnter / PHook_RollEnter).
        tst.b   PlayerV.instashield(a0)
        bne     .done

        // (2) S3K's `bclr #Status_RollJump` — unconditional in the reference, so it
        // sits ahead of the suppression test here too. d1 still carries the state
        // byte from step (0); Player_SetState clobbers d1, but d1 is dead after this
        // (step 3 writes it before reading it).
        cmpi.b  #PSTATE_ROLLJUMP, d1
        bne     .not_rolljump
        moveq   #PSTATE_JUMP, d0
        jbsr    Player_SetState                 // enter hook is PHook_AirBallEnter for
                                                // both states — idempotent, no curl
    .not_rolljump:

        // (3) the barrier predicate — always false today (see the fn's comment)
        instashield_suppressed(a0, d1)
        bne     .done

        // (4) ATTACKING, (5) the flash, (6) the sound — S3K's own order at
        // Sonic_InstaShield (sonic3k.asm:23477-23483: `move.b #1,(Shield+anim).w` /
        // `move.b #1,double_jump_flag(a0)` / `move.w #sfx_InstaAttack,d0` /
        // `jmp (Play_SFX).l`), i.e. state first, spawn second, sound as the tail.
        //
        // ONCE PER ACTIVATION, and the guarantee is step (1) above, not this line: the
        // `tst.b PlayerV.instashield / bne .done` one-shot lets ONLY the READY value
        // reach here, and the very next instruction writes ATTACKING. The flash's own
        // last animation frame writes SPENT, and only a landing (PHook_GroundEnter /
        // PHook_RollEnter) writes READY again — so between one landing and the next,
        // exactly one press can ever get past (1), and therefore exactly one SFX post
        // can ever happen. It is NOT per-animation-frame: InstaShield_Main, which runs
        // every frame of the flash, does not go through here at all.
        //
        // Sound_PlaySFX preserves a0 and clobbers only d0 — inside this proc's declared
        // d0-d2/a1-a2, and d0 is dead after InstaShield_Spawn returns.
        move.b  #INSTASHIELD_ATTACKING, PlayerV.instashield(a0)
        if SOUND_DRIVER_ENABLED == 1 {
        jbsr    InstaShield_Spawn               // clobbers d0-d2/a1, preserves a0
        move.b  #SFXID_INSTASHIELD, d0 as SfxId
        jbra    Sound_PlaySFX                   // tail, exactly as S3K's `jmp (Play_SFX).l`
        } else {
        jbra    InstaShield_Spawn               // tail — clobbers d0-d2/a1, preserves a0
        }
    .done:
        rts
}

// ===========================================================================
// THE FLASH — the effect object the ability spawns, and its data
// ===========================================================================

// -----------------------------------------------
// The blobs. Art, mappings and DPLC are EMITTED; the donor animation script is a
// comptime reference ONLY — it is bound to a `const` and never to a `pub data`, so
// it costs zero ROM bytes and exists so the gates below can COMPUTE their
// expectations from S3K instead of restating them as literals.
//
// Provenance: sonic_hack (the approved data donor). The art was already in-tree and
// is byte-identical to sonic_hack/art/uncompressed/instashield.bin; the other three
// are produced by tools/compose_instashield.py from the donor's own .asm listings.
// Reproduced and byte-compared by tools/test_instashield_art.py.
// -----------------------------------------------
const _art_insta    = embed("art/uncompressed/shields/insta_shield.bin")
const _map_insta    = embed("games/sonic4/data/mappings/insta_shield.bin")
const _dplc_insta   = embed("games/sonic4/data/dplc/insta_shield.bin")
const _donor_anim   = embed("games/sonic4/data/animations/insta_shield_donor_anim.bin")

const INSTA_ART_TILES   = 52
const INSTA_FRAMES      = 8             // S3K's Map_InstaShield offset-table length
const DONOR_ANIM_LEN    = 17            // the donor attack script, terminator included

ensure(_art_insta.len == INSTA_ART_TILES * TILE_SIZE,
       "insta_shield.bin is {_art_insta.len} bytes; the S3K sheet is {INSTA_ART_TILES} tiles")
ensure(_donor_anim.len == DONOR_ANIM_LEN,
       "insta_shield_donor_anim.bin is {_donor_anim.len} bytes, expected {DONOR_ANIM_LEN} — re-run tools/compose_instashield.py")
ensure(_map_insta.len <= $7FFF, "Map_InstaShield exceeds signed word-offset range")
ensure(_dplc_insta.len <= $7FFF, "DPLC_InstaShield exceeds signed word-offset range")
ensure((_map_insta.len & 1) == 0 && (_dplc_insta.len & 1) == 0 && (_art_insta.len & 1) == 0,
       "an insta-shield blob is odd-length — the label after it would land on an odd address")

// -----------------------------------------------
// PALETTE CENSUS — the claim "draws on Sonic's own CRAM line 0 with no remap" pinned
// to the bytes.
//
// The sonic_hack donor uses exactly {0, 6, 7, 8}: transparent, plus $0EEE / $0CAA /
// $0866 in art/palettes/SonicAndTails.bin — a white-to-blue-grey ramp, which is what
// the flash is. skdisasm's own Insta-Shield.bin is a DIFFERENT blob (855 bytes
// differ) using {0, 1, $C, $D}; on our palette those are black and the two REDS, so
// a rebuild from the wrong donor would silently render the flash in Sonic's shoe
// colours. This is the check that catches it by name.
// Flat accumulators over statement ifs, never a nested if-expression (EMP_PITFALLS §1).
// -----------------------------------------------
comptime fn insta_index_ok(v: int) -> int {
    comptime var ok = 0
    if v == 0 { ok = 1 }
    if v == 6 { ok = 1 }
    if v == 7 { ok = 1 }
    if v == 8 { ok = 1 }
    return ok
}

comptime fn count_bad_nibbles(blob: Data, len: int) -> int {
    comptime var bad = 0
    for i in 0..len {
        if insta_index_ok(blob[i] >> 4) == 0  { bad = bad + 1 }
        if insta_index_ok(blob[i] & $F) == 0  { bad = bad + 1 }
    }
    return bad
}

ensure(count_bad_nibbles(_art_insta, INSTA_ART_TILES * TILE_SIZE) == 0,
       "insta_shield.bin has {count_bad_nibbles(_art_insta, INSTA_ART_TILES * TILE_SIZE)} pixels outside the donor's index set (0,6,7,8) — regenerated from skdisasm's blob ({{0,1,$C,$D}}) instead of sonic_hack's?")

// -----------------------------------------------
// THE SCRIPT — S3K's Ani_InstaShield animation 1, and every number in the gates
// below is COMPUTED from the donor bytes rather than restated.
//
// Donor: `dc.b 0, 0,1,2,3,4,5,6,6,6,6,6,6,6,7, $FD, 0` — a duration byte of 0 and
// FOURTEEN frame bytes. (The leading `0,0` is duration-then-frame-0, not a doubled
// first frame; misreading it is how "15 frames" gets written down.) Both S3K's
// Animate_Sprite (`subq.b/bcc`) and Aeon's AnimateSprite (animate.emp:91,
// `subq.b/bpl`) hold a frame for duration+1 display frames, so the same byte
// reproduces S3K's cadence exactly.
//
// Frames 0-5 are the visible flash; mapping frames 6 and 7 are BOTH the zero-piece
// frame, so the last 8 entries draw nothing at all. That is deliberate in S3K — the
// attack window outlives the flash by more than half its length — and it is why the
// terminal entry has frame index 7 while frame 6 is what precedes it: 7 is reachable
// exactly once, which is what makes it a usable end-of-attack marker.
//
// The only substitution is the terminator: S3K's `$FD, 0` returns the persistent
// object to its invisible idle; ours is AF_DELETE, because this object is spawned per
// use and the script owns its lifetime.
// -----------------------------------------------
const INSTA_SCRIPT: [u8; 16] = [0,                                  // duration
                                0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5,                   // the visible flash
                                6, 6, 6, 6, 6, 6, 6, 7,             // the invisible tail
                                AF_DELETE]

// Display frames a script shows before its terminator, in either dialect: the frame
// bytes are everything up to the first control byte, each held (duration + 1) times.
// The AF_SET_FIELD floor ($F7) is BELOW S3K's control floor ($FC), so one walker
// serves both — and stopping at the control byte is what keeps S3K's `$FD` ARGUMENT
// (a 0, which is a legal frame index) from counting as a fifteenth frame.
pub comptime fn script_display_frames(script: Data, len: int) -> int {
    comptime var frames = 0
    comptime var stop = 0
    for i in 1..len {
        if script[i] >= AF_SET_FIELD { stop = 1 }
        if stop == 0 { frames = frames + 1 }
    }
    return frames * (script[0] + 1)
}

// The last frame index a script reaches — S3K's `cmpi.b #7,mapping_frame`, read out
// of the reference instead of copied off it.
pub comptime fn script_last_frame(script: Data, len: int) -> int {
    comptime var last = 0
    comptime var stop = 0
    for i in 1..len {
        if script[i] >= AF_SET_FIELD { stop = 1 }
        if stop == 0 { last = script[i] }
    }
    return last
}

// INSTASHIELD_LAST_FRAME is a PURE LITERAL, deliberately, and the ensure below is
// what keeps it honest. It appears in an INSTRUCTION operand (InstaShield_Main's
// end-of-attack test), and an embed-derived comptime value in that position does not
// survive the contract-closure elaboration — the instruction drops out of the
// analysis silently, which is exactly the under-approximation that gate exists to
// catch (measured here, 2026-08-26: `InstaShield_Main: 1` dropped). Same call
// ring_sparkle.emp makes for RING_SPARKLE_ART_LEN, for the same class of reason.
// The EXPECTATION is still derived: the ensure computes S3K's own last frame out of
// the embedded donor script and fails by name if this number ever stops matching it.
pub const INSTASHIELD_LAST_FRAME  = 7

ensure(INSTASHIELD_LAST_FRAME == script_last_frame(_donor_anim, DONOR_ANIM_LEN),
       "INSTASHIELD_LAST_FRAME is {INSTASHIELD_LAST_FRAME} but S3K's Ani_InstaShield ends on mapping frame {script_last_frame(_donor_anim, DONOR_ANIM_LEN)} — InstaShield_Main's end-of-attack test would never fire, and the attack window would never close")
ensure(script_display_frames(INSTA_SCRIPT, 16) == script_display_frames(_donor_anim, DONOR_ANIM_LEN),
       "the insta-shield flash shows {script_display_frames(INSTA_SCRIPT, 16)} display frames; S3K's Ani_InstaShield shows {script_display_frames(_donor_anim, DONOR_ANIM_LEN)} — re-derive the script, do not retune the reference")
ensure(script_last_frame(INSTA_SCRIPT, 16) == script_last_frame(_donor_anim, DONOR_ANIM_LEN),
       "the insta-shield script ends on mapping frame {script_last_frame(INSTA_SCRIPT, 16)}; S3K ends on {script_last_frame(_donor_anim, DONOR_ANIM_LEN)} — the end-of-attack marker would never fire")
ensure(INSTA_SCRIPT[15] == AF_DELETE,
       "the insta-shield script must end in AF_DELETE — the script owns the effect's lifetime, there is no timer field")

// -----------------------------------------------
// ZERO PIECES <=> ZERO ENTRIES — the one deviation from the donor DPLC, checked in
// BOTH directions so it can never become a claim.
//
// tools/compose_instashield.py emits a 0-entry DPLC frame for every mapping frame
// that draws 0 pieces. S3K gets there differently (its object loads a DPLC only on
// mapping_frame 0 and 3, so its trailing entries are simply never read), while
// Aeon's generic Perform_DPLC loads on every frame CHANGE — leaving the donor's
// entries there would DMA 928 dead bytes twice per insta-shield, for frames 6 and 7,
// which draw nothing.
//
// The reverse direction matters just as much: a DRAWN frame with no DPLC entries
// would render whatever the window last held.
// -----------------------------------------------
comptime fn nz(v: int) -> int {
    comptime var r = 0
    if v != 0 { r = 1 }
    return r
}

comptime fn map_frame_pieces(m: Data, f: int) -> int {
    comptime var off = (m[f << 1] << 8) | m[(f << 1) + 1]
    return (m[off + 4] << 8) | m[off + 5]        // FRAME_PIECE_COUNT is at +4
}

comptime fn dplc_frame_entries(d: Data, f: int) -> int {
    comptime var off = (d[f << 1] << 8) | d[(f << 1) + 1]
    return (d[off] << 8) | d[off + 1]
}

comptime fn zero_frame_mismatches(m: Data, d: Data, frames: int) -> int {
    comptime var bad = 0
    for f in 0..frames {
        if nz(map_frame_pieces(m, f)) != nz(dplc_frame_entries(d, f)) { bad = bad + 1 }
    }
    return bad
}

ensure((((_map_insta[0] << 8) | _map_insta[1]) >> 1) == INSTA_FRAMES,
       "Map_InstaShield has {(((_map_insta[0] << 8) | _map_insta[1]) >> 1)} frames, expected {INSTA_FRAMES}")
ensure((((_dplc_insta[0] << 8) | _dplc_insta[1]) >> 1) == INSTA_FRAMES,
       "DPLC_InstaShield has {(((_dplc_insta[0] << 8) | _dplc_insta[1]) >> 1)} frames, expected {INSTA_FRAMES} — the 3-frame blob this replaced was wrong")
ensure(zero_frame_mismatches(_map_insta, _dplc_insta, INSTA_FRAMES) == 0,
       "{zero_frame_mismatches(_map_insta, _dplc_insta, INSTA_FRAMES)} insta-shield frame(s) disagree about being empty — either art is loaded for a frame that draws nothing, or a drawn frame loads none and renders whatever the window last held")
ensure(map_frame_pieces(_map_insta, INSTASHIELD_LAST_FRAME) == 0,
       "the insta-shield's terminal frame draws {map_frame_pieces(_map_insta, INSTASHIELD_LAST_FRAME)} pieces; S3K's is empty — the flash would freeze on a visible frame for its last tick")

// -----------------------------------------------
// THE VRAM WALL — the peak comes from the DATA, and the wall from its owner.
// 928 + 29 = 957, three tiles below the character window.
// -----------------------------------------------
ensure(VRAM_INSTA_SHIELD + dplc_peak_tiles(_dplc_insta) <= VRAM_TEST_SONIC,
       "the insta-shield's peak DPLC frame overruns its VRAM window into the character window — widen games/sonic4/vram.toml's insta_shield region or re-page the art")

// Entries are DMA queue SLOTS, a quantity dplc_peak_tiles does not measure. This
// object streams on the DEFERRABLE queue (see InstaShield_Main), not the Important
// one Sonic's own DPLC saturates, so the budget is DMA_DEFERRABLE_SLOTS; the same
// DPLC_ENTRY_RESERVE headroom is kept, because the deferrable queue also carries the
// charge dust and whatever else slips there on a busy frame.
ensure(dplc_peak_entries(_dplc_insta) + DPLC_ENTRY_RESERVE <= DMA_DEFERRABLE_SLOTS,
       "the insta-shield DPLC's peak frame costs more Deferrable queue slots than the budget leaves — re-page the art")

// THE 12-BIT tile_start CEILING. Vacuously true for a 52-tile sheet (4,044 of
// headroom) and stated anyway, because the property is "every DPLC-fed art sheet
// carries this", not "the big ones do" — tools/test_dplc_tile_start.py enumerates
// the modules and refuses one that skips it. See engine/objects/dplc.emp.
ensure(_art_insta.len / TILE_SIZE <= DPLC_ADDRESSABLE_TILES,
       "Art_InstaShield is {_art_insta.len / TILE_SIZE} tiles; a DPLC entry's tile_start can only name 0..{DPLC_ADDRESSABLE_TILES - 1}, so the tiles above that would be addressed by a WRAPPED index and load early-frame art — re-page or shrink the sheet, do not raise the field")

// -----------------------------------------------
// The follower's own sst_custom overlay — just the player it tracks. NOT parent_ptr;
// see the module header and dust_spindash.emp's DustV for the three reasons.
// -----------------------------------------------
vars InstaV: Sst.sst_custom {
        player: u16,            // the player SST this flash is attached to
}

// Band = player + 1 -> IN FRONT of the character, matching S3K's priority $80 against
// the player's $100 (the conventions are inverted; the RELATIONSHIP transfers, never
// the number). config/constants.emp owns the relationship and its ensures.
// Whole-byte write into a freshly zeroed slot — nothing is inherited, so the
// clear-then-set set_priority_band idiom is not needed. RF_COORDMODE stays clear:
// world coordinates, like the player's.
const INSTA_SHIELD_RENDER_FLAGS = EFFECT_INSTA_SHIELD_BAND << RF_PRIORITY_SHIFT

// Palette 0, low priority — vram_art(tile,0,0) == tile. S3K writes
// make_art_tile(ArtTile_Shield,0,0) and then copies the player's high-priority bit;
// our player's art_tile is vram_art(cd_vrambase,0,0) with that bit CLEAR, so the copy
// would be a no-op and is not reproduced. The palette line is Sonic's own (line 0),
// which the census above proves the art belongs on.
const INSTA_SHIELD_ART_ATTR = vram_art(VRAM_INSTA_SHIELD, 0, 0)

// -----------------------------------------------
// InstaShield_Spawn — drop the flash on the player.
//
// Called (tail) from Ability_InstaShield, which has already refused the one-shot and
// the barrier predicate and written INSTASHIELD_ATTACKING.
//
// prev_anim/prev_frame = $FF is the animator-owned init documented on
// RefreshSpritePieceCount: the first AnimateSprite takes its .anim_changed path and
// populates the piece count + frame_off cache before the first render, so there is no
// PopulateSpawnedPieceCount call here.
//
// `anim` is left at the slot's zeroed 0 rather than written: S3K writes 1 because its
// table's attack script is index 1 (index 0 is the invisible idle a persistent object
// needs); ours has one row and it is the attack.
//
// In:  a0 = player SST
// Out: the flash allocated, or NOTHING if the effect pool is exhausted — the silent
//      skip every creator uses (children.emp:604). THE ATTACK STILL HAPPENS: the flag
//      was written before this call, so a flash lost to a full pool costs the visual
//      and nothing else. Dropping the new cosmetic is the unanimous policy here; do
//      not add eviction.
// Clobbers: d0 (AllocEffect's status), a1
// Preserves: a0
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc InstaShield_Spawn (a0: *Sst) clobbers(d0/a1) preserves(a0) {
        jbsr    AllocEffect             // a1 = slot on the eq edge; a1 survives the
        bne     .done                   //   pool-empty edge untouched

        move.w  #InstaShield_Main - ObjCodeBase, Sst.code_addr(a1)
        move.l  Sst.x_pos(a0), Sst.x_pos(a1)    // placed NOW, not left at 0: the Effect
        move.l  Sst.y_pos(a0), Sst.y_pos(a1)    //   sweep may render this slot before
                                                //   its first Main runs
        move.l  #Map_InstaShield, Sst.mappings(a1)
        move.w  #INSTA_SHIELD_ART_ATTR, Sst.art_tile(a1)
        move.l  #Ani_InstaShield, Sst.anim_table(a1)
        move.b  #$FF, Sst.prev_anim(a1)
        move.b  #$FF, Sst.prev_frame(a1)
        move.b  #INSTA_SHIELD_RENDER_FLAGS, Sst.render_flags(a1)
        move.w  a0, InstaV.player(a1)
    .done:
        rts
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// InstaShield_Main — follow the player, end the attack window, animate, stream, draw.
//
// Runs from the Effect sweep, which RunObjects walks AFTER the Player sweep, so the
// position and status read here are the player's FINAL values for this frame.
//
// THE END-OF-ATTACK TEST SITS BEFORE THE ANIMATOR, AND THE ORDER IS DERIVED.
// S3K tests after (`cmpi.b #7,mapping_frame` then write 2, sonic3k.asm:34613-34617)
// because its TouchResponse runs on the player BEFORE the Shield object in the same
// pass — so the frame on which the object writes 2 is still an attacking frame for
// that frame's touch test, and the window comes to 14. Aeon's level tick is the other
// way round: RunObjects (players AND effects) and then TouchResponse
// (games/sonic4/test/ojz_scroll_test.emp:597, 693), so a write made during this call
// IS visible to the same frame's touch test, and S3K's placement would give 13.
// Moving the test one call earlier — it then sees the frame the PREVIOUS call set —
// puts the write on this object's fifteenth and last call, the one where AF_DELETE
// retires it, and restores the count to exactly 14. Same reference behaviour, opposite
// instruction order, because the surrounding order is opposite.
//
// The compare-before-write is S3K's, for S3K's reason: the player may have LANDED
// under a running flash, which re-arms his flag to READY, and a landed player must not
// have SPENT written back over that fresh 0.
//
// Running AnimateSprite last is also what makes the AF_DELETE underneath it safe. The
// slot is zeroed by then, so Perform_DPLC_Deferrable reads mapping_frame == prev_frame
// == 0 and returns at its first branch, and Draw_Sprite guards null mappings
// (sprites.emp:72-74) — the same animate-then-draw-a-dead-slot path dust_puff and
// ring_sparkle already run. The player pointer is read ONCE, at the top, so nothing
// dereferences a stale a1 afterwards.
//
// NO RETIREMENT POLL, unlike the charge dust: this object's life is a fixed 15 calls
// and the script ends it, so there is no state to fall out of sync with.
//
// In:  a0 = own SST
// Clobbers: d0-d4, a1-a3 — the callee union: AnimateSprite (d0-d2/a1-a2) union
//           Perform_DPLC_Deferrable (d0-d4/a1-a2, a3 an input written here) union
//           Draw_Sprite (d0-d3/a1). a0/d7 preserved per the dispatch contract.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc InstaShield_Main (a0: *Sst) clobbers(d0-d4/a1-a3) preserves(a0) {
        movea.w InstaV.player(a0), a1

        // Position is the player's EXACTLY — the flash is centred on him, and the
        // offsets live in its mappings (S3K: `move.w x_pos(a2),x_pos(a0)`).
        move.l  Sst.x_pos(a1), Sst.x_pos(a0)
        move.l  Sst.y_pos(a1), Sst.y_pos(a0)

        // Mirror the facing only. AnimateSprite propagates status bits 1-2 into
        // render_flags itself, so copying the whole status byte would drag across bits
        // this object has no business carrying — S3K masks its own inherit to the
        // orientation bit for the same reason (`andi.b #1,status(a0)`).
        bclr    #ST_XFLIP, Sst.status(a0)
        btst    #ST_XFLIP, Sst.status(a1)
        beq     .no_flip
        bset    #ST_XFLIP, Sst.status(a0)
    .no_flip:

        // End of the attack window — see the ordering note above.
        cmpi.b  #INSTASHIELD_LAST_FRAME, Sst.mapping_frame(a0)
        bne     .not_over
        cmpi.b  #INSTASHIELD_ATTACKING, PlayerV.instashield(a1)
        bne     .not_over                       // landed under us: leave his fresh READY
        move.b  #INSTASHIELD_SPENT, PlayerV.instashield(a1)
    .not_over:

        jbsr    AnimateSprite                   // may DELETE this slot (AF_DELETE)

        movea.l #DPLC_InstaShield, a2
        movea.l #Art_InstaShield, a3
        move.w  #vram_bytes(VRAM_INSTA_SHIELD), d1
        jbsr    Perform_DPLC_Deferrable         // Deferrable, not Important: Sonic's own
                                                // DPLC already saturates the Important
                                                // queue (collision_data.emp's ratchet),
                                                // and this is cosmetic non-player art
                                                // that may slip a frame. A dropped
                                                // enqueue leaves prev_frame stale, so
                                                // the next frame RETRIES.
        jbra    Draw_Sprite
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Data — the sprite trio and the script, in this section with the code.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub offsets Ani_InstaShield {
    Attack: [u8; 16] = INSTA_SCRIPT,
}

pub data Map_InstaShield  (align: 2) = _map_insta
pub data DPLC_InstaShield (align: 2) = _dplc_insta
pub data Art_InstaShield  (align: 2) = _art_insta
